{"episodes":{"209766":"Scattered supercell thunderstorms developed near a slow-moving frontal boundary during the afternoon and evening hours on February 26. Cold temperatures were present aloft with strong deep layer shear but relatively weak low-level shear. Scattered severe hail reports were received, with the largest hailstones falling in portions of Blount, Jefferson, and St. Clair counties, as large as two inches in diameter. Damaging winds also occurred, with the most significant wind damage in Pinson where a microburst occurred. A hail storm associated with the microburst resulted in accumulating hail that covered the ground and resulted in the formation of hail fog. In addition, a brief EF-0 tornado also moved through Trussville, damaging some commercial and industrial buildings as well as homes, most of which was minor.","209804":"Multiple supercells developed during the mid to late afternoon hours, lasting into the mid evening hours.  All of these thunderstorms produced hail, some of it large.  Hail up to 2 inches in diameter was reported, and many locations reported large accumulations of hail with drifts up to a couple feet.  Strong and gusty winds in a couple of the thunderstorms produced wind driven hail damage as well.","209805":"Multiple supercells developed during the mid to late afternoon hours in north Alabama. A couple of these storms moved northeast into southern middle Tennessee during the evening hours producing large hail.","209876":"A boundary situated just south of the Tennessee and Alabama state line on February 26, 2026, was the focus for thunderstorm development during the evening hours. Most thunderstorm activity remained in north Alabama, however, one rogue thunderstorm traveled east-northeast in southern Lawrence and Giles counties where it produced quarter size hail and minor wind damage.","210534":"A cold front pushing south across Mississippi produced a sufficiently sheared environment, while cold air aloft produced steep lapse rates. Resulting thunderstorms along the front produced quarter-sized hail in portions of north central Mississippi.","210786":"With a stationary front to the south, an elevated supercell moved into Marion County and produced dime to quarter sized hail.","210863":"A stationary front across the area combined with well above normal temperatures allowed for the development of scattered showers and thunderstorms across the area. A few storms were able to become strong with one cell in north Georgia reaching severe thresholds. This cell was able to produce large hail of 1 to 1.25 inches before quickly dissipating.","210891":"A slowly sinking cold front provided lift for thunderstorm development during the evening hours across north Mississippi. Partial clearing of skies prior to the frontal arrival resulted in moderate destabilization. Climatologically, February is cooler, which increases confidence in very cold air aloft. The lift from the front, an unstable environment, and cold air aloft raised a primary concern for large hail. Elevated MUCAPE values (up to 1500 J/kg) and steep lapse rates resulted in hail up to golf ball size to occur.","211306":"A departing cold front brought winds in excess of 55 mph late on the 27th into the early hours of the 28th.","211338":"Thunderstorms produced strong wind gusts along the Space and Treasure Coasts."},"events":[{"begin":"2026-02-27T00:01Z","county":"Colbert","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2026-02-27T00:01Z","endLat":34.59,"endLon":-87.81,"episode":209804,"id":1309047,"injuries":0,"lat":34.59,"location":"0.89 NW Crooked Oak","lon":-87.81,"magnitude":2.0,"narrative":"Up to 2 inch diameter hail was measured on Wagon Mountain Road.","source":"Emergency Manager","state":"AL","type":"Hail","wfo":"HUN"},{"begin":"2026-02-27T00:01Z","county":"Franklin","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2026-02-27T00:01Z","endLat":34.56,"endLon":-87.84,"episode":209804,"id":1309049,"injurie